WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.298
Rust's safe abstractions help prevent
buffer overflows,

2
00:00:03.378 --> 00:00:05.833
but safe programs still need resource
discipline.

3
00:00:05.913 --> 00:00:09.739
A program can avoid memory corruption and
still fail because it accepts too much

4
00:00:09.819 --> 00:00:13.017
input, allocates too much memory, performs
too much parsing work,

5
00:00:13.097 --> 00:00:15.038
or waits too long on a malformed request.

6
00:00:15.118 --> 00:00:17.288
Length and capacity are different
concepts.

7
00:00:17.368 --> 00:00:19.937
Length describes how much data is
currently present.

8
00:00:20.017 --> 00:00:24.072
Capacity describes how much storage has
been allocated or can be used before growth.

9
00:00:24.152 --> 00:00:27.863
Confusing those ideas can lead to flawed
assumptions about what data exists,

10
00:00:27.943 --> 00:00:30.513
what can be written, or what a downstream
API expects.

11
00:00:30.593 --> 00:00:32.477
Untrusted size values deserve review.

12
00:00:32.557 --> 00:00:35.697
Network lengths, file sizes, protocol
fields, database values,

13
00:00:35.777 --> 00:00:39.717
and platform API results may need
validation before they influence allocation or

14
00:00:39.797 --> 00:00:40.254
parsing.

15
00:00:40.334 --> 00:00:43.189
Defensive code sets maximums, rejects
unsupported sizes,

16
00:00:43.269 --> 00:00:46.867
and avoids assuming that every conversion
between integer types is safe or

17
00:00:46.947 --> 00:00:47.575
meaningful.

18
00:00:47.655 --> 00:00:50.567
Integer sizing and conversions are common
boundary issues.

19
00:00:50.647 --> 00:00:53.388
A value may be signed in one place and
unsigned in another.

20
00:00:53.468 --> 00:00:56.608
A platform type may be wider or narrower
than an application type.

21
00:00:56.688 --> 00:00:59.829
A length may represent bytes, characters,
elements, or records.

22
00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:03.849
Reviewers should ask what the unit is and
whether the value has been checked before

23
00:01:03.929 --> 00:01:04.157
use.

24
00:01:04.237 --> 00:01:06.293
Explicit limits are part of secure design.

25
00:01:06.373 --> 00:01:09.856
Set maximum request sizes, maximum field
sizes, maximum record counts,

26
00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:12.163
and timeout expectations where
appropriate.

27
00:01:12.243 --> 00:01:14.926
When a limit is exceeded, fail safely with
a clear error.

28
00:01:15.006 --> 00:01:19.289
Resource safety complements memory safety
by protecting availability and operational

29
00:01:19.369 --> 00:01:19.940
stability.
